    "blog_name": "pgxn",
    "body": "<p>Using the new <a href=\"https://github.com/pgxn/pgxn-api/wiki/JSONP\">JSONP callback support</a> in the PGXN API, I just added a sidebar to <a href=\"http://www.justatheory.com/\" title=\"Just a Theory\">my personal blog</a> that shows a list of all my PGXN distributions. The code is simple:</p>\n\n<pre><code>&lt;div class=\"links\"&gt;\n    &lt;h2&gt;PGXN Code&lt;/h2&gt;\n    &lt;script type=\"text/javascript\"&gt;\n    function pgxn_distros(data) {\n        document.write('&lt;dl&gt;');\n        for (dist in data.releases) {\n            document.write(\n                '&lt;dt&gt;&lt;a href=http://pgxn.org/dist/' + dist +\n                '&gt;' + dist + '&lt;/a&gt;&lt;/dt&gt;' +\n                '&lt;dd&gt;' + data.releases[dist].abstract + '&lt;/dd&gt;'\n            );\n        }\n        document.write('&lt;/dl&gt;');\n    }\n  &lt;/script&gt;\n  &lt;script type=\"text/javascript\"\n     src=\"http://api.pgxn.org/user/theory.json?callback=pgxn_distros\"&gt;\n  &lt;/script&gt;\n&lt;/div&gt;\n</code></pre>\n\n<p>And the output looks like this:</p>\n\n<p><img src=\"https://64.media.tumblr.com/tumblr_ll48b4u06I1qzouis.png\" alt=\"PGXN Code\"/></p>\n\n<p>Not bad, eh? AS you can see, JSONP is dead easy to use with the JSON files served by the JSON API. Try that, CPAN!</p>",
